WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.950
Is this sentence a palindrome?

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.650
It reads the same forwards and backwards

3
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.450
once you ignore spaces and punctuation

4
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:15.220
a man, a plan, a canal, Panama.

5
00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.060
How would you check it?

6
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.040
Compare the first letter with the last

7
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.500
then the second with the second to last

8
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.130
and so on, moving inward.

9
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.977
That needs a structure where

10
00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:27.190
you can look at, and remove

11
00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:28.760
items at both ends.

12
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.030
It's called a deque

13
00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:32.870
short for double-ended queue

14
00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:34.980
and people say it like the word deck.

15
00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:37.184
In this video we'll see what

16
00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.980
a deque lets you do, how to store one

17
00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.210
and the palindrome check done with it.

18
00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.777
A deque lets you add and

19
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.289
remove at both ends.

20
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.905
Add first and add last put an item

21
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.602
at the front or at the back.

22
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.381
Remove first and remove last take an item from

23
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.350
the front or the back and return it.

24
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.294
First and last look at the two end

25
00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.930
items without removing them.

26
00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:01.130
As before

27
00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:03.633
the size and the empty check are there too

28
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.038
and removing from an empty

29
00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:06.580
deque is an error.

30
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.510
A deque contains the other two.

31
00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.870
Use only one end and you have a stack.

32
00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:13.951
Add at one end and remove from the

33
00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:15.720
other and you have a queue.

34
00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:19.090
So a deque is the most general of the three

35
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.110
linear structures in this chapter.

36
00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.639
You might expect that being more

37
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.720
flexible must cost more.

38
00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.072
It doesn't have to.

39
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.521
A good deque does all four end

40
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.490
operations in constant time.

41
00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.010
But that depends on how it's stored

42
00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.630
and the choice matters.

43
00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.400
Let's check both ways.

44
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.950
In a circular array

45
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.850
add last works exactly like enqueue

46
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:46.510
the item goes at front plus size

47
00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.220
modulo the capacity.

48
00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.680
Add first is the new move.

49
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.780
We step the front index one cell backwards

50
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.840
modulo the capacity, and put the item there.

51
00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.600
From cell zero

52
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.500
one step back lands on the last cell

53
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.890
the front wraps below zero.

54
00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.721
Remove first is the dequeue we know.

55
00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.214
Remove last just clears the cell

56
00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:11.450
at front plus size minus one

57
00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.170
and reduces the size.

58
00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:14.970
Four operations

59
00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.240
each a constant number of steps.

60
00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.379
With a chain of nodes

61
00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:20.856
add first and remove first

62
00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:22.390
are easy at the head.

63
00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:23.910
Add last is easy too

64
00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:25.900
if we keep a tail reference.

65
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.886
But remove last is a problem

66
00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.440
in a singly linked chain.

67
00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.005
We need the node before the tail

68
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.816
and the only way to find it

69
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.580
is to walk from the head.

70
00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.850
That's n minus one steps.

71
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.450
The fix is to give every node two links

72
00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.310
next, and previous.

73
00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.930
Then the tail knows its predecessor

74
00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.970
and all four operations take constant time.

75
00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.210
That's a doubly linked list

76
00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:54.320
and we'll build one in Chapter two.

77
00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.850
Now the palindrome check.

78
00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:59.010
First, put every letter or digit

79
00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.810
in lower case, into a deque.

80
00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.260
Spaces and punctuation are skipped.

81
00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:05.900
Then repeat

82
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.620
take the first letter off the front

83
00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.740
and the last letter off the back.

84
00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.647
If they differ, it is not a palindrome.

85
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.338
If they always agree until fewer

86
00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.890
than two letters are left, it is.

87
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.110
Each letter is handled once

88
00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:21.272
with constant work

89
00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.518
so the whole check takes time proportional

90
00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.480
to the length of the text.

91
00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:27.433
To sum up.

92
00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.188
A deque is a queue you can

93
00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.561
use from both ends

94
00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:32.699
and it contains the stack and

95
00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:34.460
the queue as special cases.

96
00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.639
A circular array gives all four end

97
00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.420
operations in constant time

98
00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.642
because the front can step backwards.

99
00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.129
A singly linked chain can't remove

100
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.260
at the tail quickly

101
00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.810
so a linked deque needs two links per node.

102
00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.650
Next, the code

103
00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:53.360
and the standard library version.

104
00:03:53.987 --> 00:03:56.947
Now the deque, in Python.

105
00:03:57.527 --> 00:03:59.416
The circular deque has the same

106
00:03:59.477 --> 00:04:03.017
three fields as the queue: a list, a size

107
00:04:03.017 --> 00:04:05.237
and a front index.

108
00:04:05.237 --> 00:04:07.927
Add last is the queue's enqueue.

109
00:04:08.277 --> 00:04:10.227
Add first is the new line.

110
00:04:10.227 --> 00:04:13.347
It sets front to front minus one

111
00:04:13.347 --> 00:04:14.847
modulo the length of the list.

112
00:04:14.847 --> 00:04:15.947
In Python

113
00:04:16.047 --> 00:04:18.413
the percent operator always returns a result

114
00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:20.707
between zero and length minus one

115
00:04:20.707 --> 00:04:22.147
so from zero

116
00:04:22.147 --> 00:04:24.717
minus one wraps to the last cell.

117
00:04:25.067 --> 00:04:27.104
Remove first and remove last

118
00:04:27.177 --> 00:04:28.777
are the mirror images

119
00:04:28.777 --> 00:04:30.672
one moves the front forward

120
00:04:30.737 --> 00:04:32.653
the other just clears the last

121
00:04:32.717 --> 00:04:34.587
cell and reduces the size.

122
00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:38.217
Let's run it with a capacity of four.

123
00:04:38.217 --> 00:04:41.277
Add last B puts B in cell zero.

124
00:04:41.277 --> 00:04:44.177
Add first A moves the front to cell three

125
00:04:44.177 --> 00:04:48.137
and puts A there: the list reads B, None

126
00:04:48.137 --> 00:04:51.647
None, A, with front three and size two.

127
00:04:51.997 --> 00:04:55.327
Add last C and D fill the two empty cells

128
00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:56.972
so the deque is full.

129
00:04:57.047 --> 00:04:59.853
Now remove first returns A and

130
00:04:59.947 --> 00:05:03.287
remove last returns D, leaving B and C

131
00:05:03.287 --> 00:05:05.557
with the front back at zero.

132
00:05:06.137 --> 00:05:07.839
Both add operations check

133
00:05:07.907 --> 00:05:09.627
for a full list first.

134
00:05:09.627 --> 00:05:12.339
If it is full, they call the same resize

135
00:05:12.407 --> 00:05:13.547
we met with the queue

136
00:05:13.547 --> 00:05:15.167
copy the items in order

137
00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:16.287
starting from the front

138
00:05:16.287 --> 00:05:18.247
into a list twice as long

139
00:05:18.247 --> 00:05:20.097
and reset the front to zero.

140
00:05:20.447 --> 00:05:23.213
So a deque never refuses an item

141
00:05:23.297 --> 00:05:25.035
and the amortized cost of an

142
00:05:25.097 --> 00:05:26.717
add is still constant

143
00:05:26.717 --> 00:05:28.407
for the same reason as before.

144
00:05:28.757 --> 00:05:31.147
Two small methods, first and last

145
00:05:31.147 --> 00:05:32.927
just read the cell at the front

146
00:05:32.927 --> 00:05:36.127
and the cell at front plus size minus one

147
00:05:36.127 --> 00:05:38.157
again modulo the length.

148
00:05:38.737 --> 00:05:40.873
Here is the palindrome check.

149
00:05:40.947 --> 00:05:44.603
We lower-case the text and add each

150
00:05:44.707 --> 00:05:47.327
letter or digit to the deque.

151
00:05:47.327 --> 00:05:50.027
Then, while at least two letters remain

152
00:05:50.027 --> 00:05:53.137
we remove one from each end and compare.

153
00:05:53.487 --> 00:05:55.297
Racecar gives True.

154
00:05:55.297 --> 00:05:57.897
The Panama sentence gives True.

155
00:05:57.897 --> 00:05:59.437
And stack gives False

156
00:05:59.437 --> 00:06:01.097
because the first letter, s

157
00:06:01.097 --> 00:06:03.007
is not the last letter, k.

158
00:06:03.587 --> 00:06:07.577
Python's collections dot deque is a deque.

159
00:06:07.577 --> 00:06:11.237
Appendleft and append add at the two ends

160
00:06:11.237 --> 00:06:14.237
and popleft and pop remove from them.

161
00:06:14.237 --> 00:06:16.667
All four take constant time.

162
00:06:17.017 --> 00:06:19.547
It also accepts a maximum length.

163
00:06:19.547 --> 00:06:20.680
When it is full

164
00:06:20.747 --> 00:06:22.958
adding at one end silently drops

165
00:06:23.027 --> 00:06:24.396
an item from the other end

166
00:06:24.447 --> 00:06:26.113
which is handy for keeping only

167
00:06:26.167 --> 00:06:27.537
the most recent items.

168
00:06:28.117 --> 00:06:31.047
Next, the last video of the chapter

169
00:06:31.047 --> 00:06:33.287
two classic applications

170
00:06:33.287 --> 00:06:35.128
bracket matching with a stack

171
00:06:35.187 --> 00:06:37.381
and a first look at breadth-first

172
00:06:37.447 --> 00:06:38.957
search with a queue.

